and then indeed Zeus a grievous return in his heart devised
for the Argives, since not all were sensible nor just,
all of them;

There indeed Menelaus urged all the Achaeans
to take thought of their return over the broad back of the sea,
nor to Agamemnon at all was it pleasing;

for he wished, you see,
to hold back the people and to offer sacred hecatombs,
so that he might appease Athena's dread anger,
fool, nor did he know this, that he was not to persuade her;

Some, having turned back their curved-on-both-sides ships, went
with lord Odysseus, wise-minded, of many-colored cunning,
again to Atreus' son Agamemnon showing favor;

and the ships very swiftly
ran through the fish-filled paths, and to Geraestus
by night put in to land;

It was the fourth day, when at Argos their well-balanced ships
the companions of Tydeus' son, horse-taming Diomedes,
were mooring;

“O Nestor, son of Neleus, great glory of the Achaeans,
very greatly indeed did that man take vengeance, and for him the Achaeans
will carry wide fame, even for those yet to be, to hear of;
